  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A gas station offers two types of gasoline, regular and premium. On a certain day, of the $43$ customers who bought regular gas, $23$ paid with a credit card. On the same day, of the $38$ customers that bought premium gas, $31$ of them paid with a credit card. What is the probability of a randomly selected customer paying with a credit card?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person paid with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person bought regular gas. Then ${R}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the person bought premium gas.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap {R}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(C\\cap R),$ we take the number of people who bought regular gas and paid with a credit card $(23)$ and divide by the total number of customers on that day $(43+38=81).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap R)=\\frac{23}{81}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(C\\cap {R}^{\u2032}),$ we take the number of people who bought premium gas and paid with a credit card $(31)$ and divide by the total number of customers on that day $(81).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(C\\cap {R}^{\u2032})=\\frac{31}{81}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap {R}^{\u2032}) \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{81}+\\frac{31}{81} \\\\  & =\\frac{54}{81} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
